.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/ignore Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
LinuxOS XWindowsCoverageDownloads\fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP 
.SH "ignore"
.PP
\fRignore\fP is a manager, filter and parser which implemented in pure JavaScript according to the \fR\&.gitignore spec 2\&.22\&.1\fP\&.

.PP
\fRignore\fP is used by eslint, gitbook and \fRmany others\fP\&.

.PP
Pay \fBATTENTION\fP that \fR\fRminimatch\fP\fP (which used by \fRfstream-ignore\fP) does not follow the gitignore spec\&.

.PP
To filter filenames according to a \&.gitignore file, I recommend this npm package, \fRignore\fP\&.

.PP
To parse an \fR\&.npmignore\fP file, you should use \fRminimatch\fP, because an \fR\&.npmignore\fP file is parsed by npm using \fRminimatch\fP and it does not work in the \&.gitignore way\&.
.SS "Tested on"
\fRignore\fP is fully tested, and has more than \fBfive hundreds\fP of unit tests\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Linux + Node: \fR0\&.8\fP - \fR7\&.x\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Windows + Node: \fR0\&.10\fP - \fR7\&.x\fP, node < \fR0\&.10\fP is not tested due to the lack of support of appveyor\&.
.PP

.PP
Actually, \fRignore\fP does not rely on any versions of node specially\&.

.PP
Since \fR4\&.0\&.0\fP, ignore will no longer support \fRnode < 6\fP by default, to use in node < 6, \fRrequire('ignore/legacy')\fP\&. For details, see \fRCHANGELOG\fP\&.
.SS "Table Of Main Contents"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBUsage\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB`Pathname` Conventions\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
See Also:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR\fRglob-gitignore\fP\fP matches files using patterns and filters them according to gitignore rules\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBUpgrade Guide\fP
.PP
.SS "Install"
.PP
.nf
npm i ignore
.fi
.PP
.SS "Usage"
.PP
.nf
import ignore from 'ignore'
const ig = ignore()\&.add(['\&.abc/*', '!\&.abc/d/'])
.fi
.PP
.SS "Filter the given paths"
.PP
.nf
const paths = [
  '\&.abc/a\&.js',    // filtered out
  '\&.abc/d/e\&.js'   // included
]

ig\&.filter(paths)        // ['\&.abc/d/e\&.js']
ig\&.ignores('\&.abc/a\&.js') // true
.fi
.PP
.SS "As the filter function"
.PP
.nf
paths\&.filter(ig\&.createFilter()); // ['\&.abc/d/e\&.js']
.fi
.PP
.SS "Win32 paths will be handled"
.PP
.nf
ig\&.filter(['\&.abc\\\\a\&.js', '\&.abc\\\\d\\\\e\&.js'])
// if the code above runs on windows, the result will be
// ['\&.abc\\\\d\\\\e\&.js']
.fi
.PP
.SS "Why another ignore?"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRignore\fP is a standalone module, and is much simpler so that it could easy work with other programs, unlike \fRisaacs\fP's \fRfstream-ignore\fP which must work with the modules of the fstream family\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRignore\fP only contains utility methods to filter paths according to the specified ignore rules, so
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRignore\fP never try to find out ignore rules by traversing directories or fetching from git configurations\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRignore\fP don't cares about sub-modules of git projects\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Exactly according to \fRgitignore man page\fP, fixes some known matching issues of fstream-ignore, such as:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
'\fR/*\&.js\fP' should only match '\fRa\&.js\fP', but not '\fRabc/a\&.js\fP'\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
'\fR**/foo\fP' should match '\fRfoo\fP' anywhere\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Prevent re-including a file if a parent directory of that file is excluded\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Handle trailing whitespaces:
.IP "    \(bu" 6
\fR'a '\fP(one space) should not match \fR'a '\fP(two spaces)\&.
.IP "    \(bu" 6
\fR'a \\ '\fP matches \fR'a '\fP
.PP

.IP "  \(bu" 4
All test cases are verified with the result of \fRgit check-ignore\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
.SH "Methods"
.PP
.SS "\&.add(pattern: string | Ignore): this"
.SS "\&.add(patterns: Array<string | Ignore>): this"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBpattern\fP \fRString | Ignore\fP An ignore pattern string, or the \fRIgnore\fP instance
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBpatterns\fP \fRArray<String | Ignore>\fP Array of ignore patterns\&.
.PP

.PP
Adds a rule or several rules to the current manager\&.

.PP
Returns \fRthis\fP

.PP
Notice that a line starting with \fR'#'\fP(hash) is treated as a comment\&. Put a backslash (\fR'\\'\fP) in front of the first hash for patterns that begin with a hash, if you want to ignore a file with a hash at the beginning of the filename\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
ignore()\&.add('#abc')\&.ignores('#abc')    // false
ignore()\&.add('\\\\#abc')\&.ignores('#abc')   // true
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fRpattern\fP could either be a line of ignore pattern or a string of multiple ignore patterns, which means we could just \fRignore()\&.add()\fP the content of a ignore file:

.PP
.PP
.nf
ignore()
\&.add(fs\&.readFileSync(filenameOfGitignore)\&.toString())
\&.filter(filenames)
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fRpattern\fP could also be an \fRignore\fP instance, so that we could easily inherit the rules of another \fRIgnore\fP instance\&.
.SS "\&.addIgnoreFile(path)"
REMOVED in \fR3\&.x\fP for now\&.

.PP
To upgrade \fRignore@2\&.x\fP up to \fR3\&.x\fP, use

.PP
.PP
.nf
import fs from 'fs'

if (fs\&.existsSync(filename)) {
  ignore()\&.add(fs\&.readFileSync(filename)\&.toString())
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
instead\&.
.SS "\&.filter(paths: Array<Pathname>): Array<Pathname>"
.PP
.nf
type Pathname = string
.fi
.PP

.PP
Filters the given array of pathnames, and returns the filtered array\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBpaths\fP \fRArray\&.<Pathname>\fP The array of \fRpathname\fPs to be filtered\&.
.PP
.SS "\fRPathname\fP Conventions:"
.SS "1\&. \fRPathname\fP should be a \fRpath\&.relative()\fPd pathname"
\fRPathname\fP should be a string that have been \fRpath\&.join()\fPed, or the return value of \fRpath\&.relative()\fP to the current directory,

.PP
.PP
.nf
// WRONG, an error will be thrown
ig\&.ignores('\&./abc')

// WRONG, for it will never happen, and an error will be thrown
// If the gitignore rule locates at the root directory,
// `'/abc'` should be changed to `'abc'`\&.
// 
.fi
.PP
 // path\&.relative('/', '/abc') -> 'abc' // ``` ig\&.ignores('/abc')

.PP
// WRONG, that it is an absolute path on Windows, an error will be thrown ig\&.ignores('C:\\abc')

.PP
// Right ig\&.ignores('abc')

.PP
// Right ig\&.ignores(path\&.join('\&./abc')) // path\&.join('\&./abc') -> 'abc' 
.PP
.nf
In other words, each `Pathname` here should be a relative path to the directory of the gitignore rules\&.

Suppose the dir structure is:

.fi
.PP
 /path/to/your/repo |-- a | |-- a\&.js | |-- \&.b | |-- \&.c |-- \&.DS_store 
.PP
.nf
Then the `paths` might be like this:

.fi
.PP
 js [ 'a/a\&.js' '\&.b', '\&.c/\&.DS_store' ] 
.PP
.nf
#### 2\&. filenames and dirnames

`node\-ignore` does NO `fs\&.stat` during path matching, so for the example below:

.fi
.PP
 js // First, we add a ignore pattern to ignore a directory ig\&.add('config/')

.PP
// \fRig\fP does NOT know if 'config', in the real world, // is a normal file, directory or something\&.

.PP
ig\&.ignores('config') // \fRig\fP treats \fRconfig\fP as a file, so it returns \fRfalse\fP

.PP
ig\&.ignores('config/') // returns \fRtrue\fP 
.PP
.nf
Specially for people who develop some library based on `node\-ignore`, it is important to understand that\&.

Usually, you could use [`glob`](http://npmjs\&.org/package/glob) with `option\&.mark = true` to fetch the structure of the current directory:

.fi
.PP
 js import glob from 'glob'

.PP
glob('**', { // Adds a / character to directory matches\&. mark: true }, (err, files) => { if (err) { return console\&.error(err) }

.PP
let filtered = ignore()\&.add(patterns)\&.filter(files) console\&.log(filtered) }) 
.PP
.nf
## \&.ignores(pathname: Pathname): boolean

> new in 3\&.2\&.0

Returns `Boolean` whether `pathname` should be ignored\&.

.fi
.PP
 js ig\&.ignores('\&.abc/a\&.js') // true 
.PP
.nf
## \&.createFilter()

Creates a filter function which could filter an array of paths with `Array\&.prototype\&.filter`\&.

Returns `function(path)` the filter function\&.

## \&.test(pathname: Pathname) since 5\&.0\&.0

Returns `TestResult`

.fi
.PP
 ts interface TestResult { ignored: boolean // true if the \fRpathname\fP is finally unignored by some negative pattern unignored: boolean } 
.PP
.nf
\- `{ignored: true, unignored: false}`: the `pathname` is ignored
\- `{ignored: false, unignored: true}`: the `pathname` is unignored
\- `{ignored: false, unignored: false}`: the `pathname` is never matched by any ignore rules\&.

## static `ignore\&.isPathValid(pathname): boolean` since 5\&.0\&.0

Check whether the `pathname` is an valid `path\&.relative()`d path according to the [convention](#1\-pathname\-should\-be\-a\-pathrelatived\-pathname)\&.

This method is **NOT** used to check if an ignore pattern is valid\&.

.fi
.PP
 js ignore\&.isPathValid('\&./foo') // false 
.PP
.nf
## ignore(options)

### `options\&.ignorecase` since 4\&.0\&.0

Similar as the `core\&.ignorecase` option of [git\-config](https://git\-scm\&.com/docs/git\-config), `node\-ignore` will be case insensitive if `options\&.ignorecase` is set to `true` (the default value), otherwise case sensitive\&.

.fi
.PP
 js const ig = ignore({ ignorecase: false })

.PP
ig\&.add('*\&.png')

.PP
ig\&.ignores('*\&.PNG') // false 
.PP
.nf
### `options\&.ignoreCase?: boolean` since 5\&.2\&.0

Which is alternative to `options\&.ignoreCase`

### `options\&.allowRelativePaths?: boolean` since 5\&.2\&.0

This option brings backward compatibility with projects which based on `ignore@4\&.x`\&. If `options\&.allowRelativePaths` is `true`, `ignore` will not check whether the given path to be tested is [`path\&.relative()`d](#pathname\-conventions)\&.

However, passing a relative path, such as `'\&./foo'` or `'\&.\&./foo'`, to test if it is ignored or not is not a good practise, which might lead to unexpected behavior

.fi
.PP
 js ignore({ allowRelativePaths: true })\&.ignores('\&.\&./foo/bar\&.js') // And it will not throw 
.PP
.nf
****

# Upgrade Guide

## Upgrade 4\&.x \-> 5\&.x

Since `5\&.0\&.0`, if an invalid `Pathname` passed into `ig\&.ignores()`, an error will be thrown, unless `options\&.allowRelative = true` is passed to the `Ignore` factory\&.

While `ignore < 5\&.0\&.0` did not make sure what the return value was, as well as

.fi
.PP
 ts \&.ignores(pathname: Pathname): boolean

.PP
\&.filter(pathnames: Array<Pathname>): Array<Pathname>

.PP
\&.createFilter(): (pathname: Pathname) => boolean

.PP
\&.test(pathname: Pathname): {ignored: boolean, unignored: boolean} 
.PP
.nf
See the convention [here](#1\-pathname\-should\-be\-a\-pathrelatived\-pathname) for details\&.

If there are invalid pathnames, the conversion and filtration should be done by users\&.

.fi
.PP
 js import {isPathValid} from 'ignore' // introduced in 5\&.0\&.0

.PP
const paths = [ // invalid ////////////////// '', false, '\&.\&./foo', '\&.', //////////////////

.PP
// valid 'foo' ] \&.filter(isValidPath)

.PP
ig\&.filter(paths) 
.PP
.nf
## Upgrade 3\&.x \-> 4\&.x

Since `4\&.0\&.0`, `ignore` will no longer support node < 6, to use `ignore` in node < 6:

.fi
.PP
 js var ignore = require('ignore/legacy') ```
.SS "Upgrade 2\&.x -> 3\&.x"
.IP "\(bu" 2
All \fRoptions\fP of 2\&.x are unnecessary and removed, so just remove them\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRignore()\fP instance is no longer an \fR\fREventEmitter\fP\fP, and all events are unnecessary and removed\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.addIgnoreFile()\fP is removed, see the \fB\&.addIgnoreFile\fP section for details\&.
.PP

